<interrogationes unitas="tabula_characterum">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I create a text grid and type a character into it the way an editor would?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_initiare tabula_inserere_characterem tabula_cellula" via="lib/vim.c">tabula_initiare(&amp;tabula,
      piscina, latitudo, altitudo) allocates the cells (row-major) and
      the per-line indentation array from the piscina; it has no return
      value (piscina_allocare exits on failure). tabula_inserere_characterem(&amp;tabula,
      linea, columna, c) is the typing operation: in blank space it
      paints, at or on content it pushes the rest of the line right and
      wraps the overflow onto the next line. Read and write raw cells
      with the tabula_cellula(t, linea, columna) macro. The grid has no
      cursor; lib/vim.c keeps one.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does an empty cell contain, a space or a NUL?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_initiare tabula_trahere_sinistram tabula_est_cellula_vacua tabula_invenire_finem_contenti" via="lib/scriba_documentum.worklog.md">Usually
      a space: tabula_initiare, tabula_inserere_lineam and
      tabula_delere_lineam fill with ' ' and set indentation to -1 (the
      header's "initialized to '\0'" is wrong). But tabula_trahere_sinistram,
      and so tabula_delere_characterem, writes '\0' into the last
      column. tabula_est_cellula_vacua treats ' ', '\0', '\t' and
      TAB_CONTINUATIO as blank; tabula_invenire_finem_contenti treats
      only ' ' and '\0' as blank, so tabs count as content there.
      Compare a cell with tabula_est_cellula_vacua, not with ' '.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the barrier (obex) and when does typing push text instead of overwriting?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_invenire_obicem tabula_inserere_characterem">The
      obex is the first non-blank column at or after the given column
      (tabula_invenire_obicem, -1 if none). tabula_inserere_characterem
      only paints when there is no content to the right, or when at
      least one blank cell lies between the column and the obex (the
      whitespace "cushion" is used up). When the column is at the obex
      or right before it, the rest of the line is pushed one cell right.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I treat a FALSUM from tabula_inserere_characterem as "nothing happened"?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_inserere_characterem tabula_tractare_overflow tabula_inserere_tab tabula_inserere_spatium tabula_est_plena tabula_inserere_lineam">No.
      FALSUM means the page is full, but the grid has already changed:
      the line was pushed and the new character painted before the
      overflow was handled, and on the last line (or when
      tabula_tractare_overflow cannot insert a line) the pushed-out
      character is simply lost. The same holds for tabula_inserere_tab
      and tabula_inserere_spatium. tabula_est_plena (content in the last
      column of the last line) does not predict every failure: when the
      overflow must open a new line before an indented line,
      tabula_inserere_lineam refuses as soon as the last line has any
      content at all. Keep a copy of the cells if you need to undo.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I insert and delete a tab without leaving half of it behind?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_inserere_tab tabula_delere_tab tabula_est_tab_continuatio TAB_CONTINUATIO">A
      tab is two cells: '\t' followed by TAB_CONTINUATIO (0x01). Insert
      it with tabula_inserere_tab (column at most latitudo - 2) and
      remove it with tabula_delere_tab, which accepts either cell.
      tabula_delere_characterem knows nothing about tabs and removes
      only one of the two cells, so look at the cell first (for '\t'
      or TAB_CONTINUATIO) as lib/vim.c's backspace does. Pushing text off the end of a line can
      also drop the TAB_CONTINUATIO and leave a lone '\t'.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the indentatio array and who keeps it up to date?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_initiare tabula_inserere_lineam tabula_delere_lineam tabula_ex_literis_cum_dimensionibus" via="lib/vim.c">Sticky
      indentation per line (s32, -1 = not set). The library only moves
      it along with tabula_inserere_lineam / tabula_delere_lineam and
      sets it in the tabula_ex_literis helpers (the first column that is
      neither a space nor a tab). Typing never updates it: lib/vim.c sets it after a
      successful tabula_inserere_characterem. tabula_tractare_overflow
      decides indentation from the cells (tabula_invenire_initium_contenti),
      not from this array.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I write a test that checks the grid against expected text?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_ex_literis tabula_ex_literis_cum_dimensionibus tabula_aequalis_literis tabula_asserere tabula_imprimere">Build
      the start state with tabula_ex_literis (default LXVIII x LVI) or
      tabula_ex_literis_cum_dimensionibus ('\n' = next line, '\t'
      becomes the two-cell tab), act, then tabula_asserere(&amp;t,
      piscina, expectatum, descriptio). It calls CREDO_VERUM and, on
      failure, prints both grids with tabula_imprimere ('.' for '\0',
      '_' for space, '&gt;' for tab). The comparison is strict for
      characters you list (a ' ' you write does not match a '\0' cell)
      and only requires blank cells, in the tabula_est_cellula_vacua
      sense, after them.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I copy text in and out of a grid region without the push logic?
    <responsum symbolum="tabula_scribere_ad_positionem tabula_legere_ad_positionem tabula_inserere_spatium" via="lib/concha.c">tabula_scribere_ad_positionem
      overwrites cells from a NUL-terminated string and returns how many
      it wrote (it stops at the line end; tabs are not expanded).
      tabula_legere_ad_positionem copies longitudo cells into a buffer
      that must hold longitudo + 1 bytes, NUL-terminated. To make room
      first, tabula_inserere_spatium pushes the line right N times.
      lib/concha.c's $date command uses all three.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
